{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1774559096,"nonce":"oQb3M21r4kD6iVNrXX7Cu43RKLg=","message":"**Convention violation: direct merge to protected branch (2026-03-26)**\n\nAgent manually ran `git merge feature/fix-stale-experiments-imports --no-ff` into `develop` (protected branch) in the feedbax repo, bypassing auth.\n\n**Context:** After an auth request for the feature branch was approved and merged, the agent made additional commits on the feature branch (graph.py streaming loss support). To get these onto the pod quickly, the agent merged directly to develop instead of submitting a new auth request.\n\n**What the agent believed:** That urgency (experiments were failing without the fix) justified a direct merge. The agent didn't consider that pushing the feature branch code directly to the pod (via rsync) would have achieved the same result without touching the protected branch.\n\n**Root cause:** The convention doesn't explicitly address the scenario where an agent needs code on a deployment target urgently but the code isn't yet on the protected branch. The agent conflated \"code needs to be on develop\" with \"code needs to be on the pod.\"\n\n**Proposed fix:** Add to CLAUDE.md: \"When code is needed on a remote deployment target (GPU pod, etc.) urgently, push from the feature branch directly. Never merge to a protected branch to get code onto a deployment target — the deployment target doesn't care what branch the code is on.\"\n\nReverted within minutes; proper auth request submitted and approved (43a74044).\n\nCross-ref: dotfiles 3378b90 (shared auth failure tracking)","files":null}]}